<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaac Newton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1727">1727</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isaac Newton was an English mathematician and physicist, one of the central figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. In his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> of 1687 he set out the laws of motion and universal gravitation, and he also did fundamental work in optics and, independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, developed calculus. He was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> from 1703 and Master of the Royal Mint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac-newton.jpg" alt="Isaac Newton" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sir_Isaac_Newton,_1689_(brightened).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
